---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { loadTheses } from '../lib/data';
import { url } from '../lib/format';

const theses = await loadTheses();
const open = theses.filter((t) => t.status === 'open').length;
---

<Base>
	<div class="prose">
		<h1>Thesis Tracker</h1>
		<p class="lead">
			A public, timestamped record of my investment theses — every call I post, tracked against market prices and kept
			on the record whether it worked or not.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href={url('theses/')}>Browse all {theses.length} {theses.length === 1 ? 'thesis' : 'theses'}</a> ({open} open) ·{' '}
			<a href={url('scorecard/')}>See the scorecard</a>
		</p>

		<h2>How it works</h2>
		<p>
			Each thesis is a thread, like an email to a team. The opening post sets out the view; replies follow as it
			develops — after earnings, a policy decision, or whatever the catalyst was — and a final post closes it.
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Terms up front.</strong> Every opening post states the instruments, long or short, the entry date, and
				optionally a target, a horizon, and what would prove it wrong.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Append-only.</strong> A published post is never edited. New information goes in a new, dated reply. Typo
				fixes are allowed only as separate, labelled changes, and the full edit history is public on GitHub.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Every thesis gets closed</strong> as won, lost, invalidated (the "wrong if" happened) or expired (the
				horizon passed), with a closing post explaining why.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Nothing is typed by hand.</strong> Returns and the scorecard are computed from the posts and saved market
				prices every time the site is built.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>How returns are measured</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<strong>Entry point</strong> is the closing price on the entry date. I choose that date, usually the close my
				analysis was based on, and the site always shows how many days it was before the post.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Total return</strong> (dividends reinvested on the ex-date, adjusted for splits) is the headline figure;
				price-only return is shown alongside.
			</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Pairs and baskets</strong> are measured over gross position size: total profit and loss divided by the
				sum of all long and short sizes. A short pays the dividends on what it's short.
			</li>
			<li><strong>Yield theses</strong> (e.g. a view on the 10-year Treasury yield) are measured in basis points, never mixed with % returns.</li>
			<li><strong>Targets</strong> count as reached on the first close at or beyond the target after the entry date.</li>
			<li>
				<strong>Prices</strong> are daily closes from Yahoo Finance, saved into the public repository when fetched and
				never overwritten afterwards.
			</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>How it's proven</h2>
		<p>
			Git commit dates can be set to anything on my own machine, so they aren't proof on their own. Each post is also
			anchored by independent third parties:
		</p>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>OpenTimestamps</strong> — a cryptographic fingerprint of each post is recorded in the Bitcoin blockchain, which nobody can backdate.</li>
			<li><strong>Wayback Machine</strong> — the Internet Archive keeps its own copy of each thesis page as it looked when published.</li>
			<li><strong>Public history</strong> — every change to every file is visible on GitHub.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>The <a href={url('verify/')}>verification guide</a> shows how to check any of this yourself.</p>

		<h2>What this doesn't cover</h2>
		<p>
			This is a record of the theses I chose to post. It can't show ideas I had and never published. What it does
			guarantee is that once something is posted, it stays — with its result — and can't be changed or removed without
			that being visible.
		</p>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.prose {
		max-width: 44rem;
	}
	.lead {
		font-size: 1.15rem;
	}
	h2 {
		font-size: 1.15rem;
		margin: 2rem 0 0.5rem;
	}
	li {
		margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
	}
</style>
